---
description: Prefer dictionaries over object and cast
alwaysApply: true
---

# No `object` and cast

Do not take `object` (or `object?`) and cast it to a dictionary, options type, or domain type unless there is no other API. Model options as a dictionary. Typed connection options stay on constructors, not on factory `Create`.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
IDatasource Create(object? options = null) =>
    options is SqliteConnectionOptions typed
        ? new SqliteDatasource(typed)
        : new SqliteDatasource(new SqliteConnectionOptions
        {
            Filename = DatasourceOptions.Value(options, "filename")
        });

// ✅ GOOD
IDatasource Create(IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string?>? options = null) =>
    new SqliteDatasource(new SqliteConnectionOptions
    {
        Filename = Get(options, "filename") ?? SqlitePath.FromUrl(Get(options, "url"))
    });
```

Last resort only: a host or serializer that truly hands you `object`. Cast once at that boundary to a named dictionary or type; do not thread `object` through the public API.
